## The three asks
1. **s04 (0:15.4–0:21.4), `s04_wan1`: pass.** 1 held frame and 1 jump event in the whole shot (cut-v6b: 3 and 5); speed 0.22 px/f (below my ~0.3 noise limit). On frames the girl keeps her
   match-offering pose through "who will buy my matches?", passers-by walk past in depth (a woman in front, a man behind, no merging), and she stands alone for "No one had bought a single match." (`frames/s04.png`, `s04_merge.png`).
2. **s15/s16 (my editor#15): pass.** s15 now ends at 1:32.21 on the blaze; the cream-costume wide is gone; the grandmother is first seen in s16 in the lavender-grey dress and maroon shawl as in s14 and
   the rest of s16. The strike sync (s15 1:30.58–1:31.4) is untouched. Two notes: (a) the embrace now starts at 1:32.21, 2.4 s before the narration says "Grandmother took her in her arms" (cue at 1:34.59); the cut
   is blaze → white wash, so it reads as the grandmother answering the strike, but the picture leads the words; (b) because s16 is now 10.04 s long its take runs to its end: the rise eases to rest in the last
   ~1.3 s (frame-to-frame difference 5 → 1 → 0.2–0.5 luma from 1:39 to 1:42.2; speed 0.15 px/f at 1:40, 0.01 at 1:41; only the snow moves), where cut-v6b kept 1 px/f. No repeated-frame fault
   (4 isolated single repeats in s16, none in the last second; the near-still last second is snow only).
3. **s14 and s18 cadence: improved, with a cost.** s14: ripple 0.333 → 0.090, jump events 2 → 0, no held frame; s18: ripple 0.441 → 0.182, 5 → 1 jump. The motion-compensated in-betweens are
   visibly softer than the kept frames: the sharpness (Laplacian variance) of the reaching hand in s14 is 393 on even frames and 253 on odd frames (odd/even 0.64; cut-v6b 1.03), with a ghosted double edge on the
   fingers on the odd frames (`frames/s14_consec.png`: tiles 2, 4, 6); s18 odd/even 0.86; in s18 I saw no tearing on the woman's hand or the bonnet (`frames/s18_consec.png`). This is a 12 Hz soft/sharp alternation
   on a moving hand: not a tear, and less distracting than the old every-other-frame stepping in my judgement, but it is an artefact the director should see at full size.

